00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:50,000
This past week one of the posts on the blog was about simple versus complicated scouting.

34
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Rebecca wrote in to say I love the post.

35
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I have two Girl Scout troops and this thinking transfers to Girl Scouts as well.

36
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
I could not agree more.

37
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Scoutmaster Run commented on that same post.

38
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
He says: you change a boy's perspective this way:

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
A boy packs his pack,

40
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,000
His patrol goes in the woods.

41
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
They set up camp, collect firewood, build campfires,

42
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,000
They cook food.

43
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
They thank their creator for the food

44
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
They eat together.

45
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:16,000
They clean up,

46
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,000
They de-literate trail,

47
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:18,000
They observe nature.

48
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:19,000
They hike in the forest,

49
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
They count stars, tell jokes around the campfire, sleep intents.

50
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,000
They clean up the site,

51
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:25,000
They head home.

52
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The boy unpacks, checks, cleans and stores his gear.

53
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
In this simple process we see the growth of cooperation, fellowship, leadership,

54
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
service, reverence, planning, preparedness, knowledge, self-reliance and confidence.

55
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
It's pretty straightforward, pretty simple and pretty effective.

56
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Well, Ron, I couldn't agree with you more.

57
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
