64
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
So changing the post to include venturing, exploring and sea scouts would be wonderful.

65
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Thank you for your work on the behalf of the BSA and hopefully, venturing, exploring and sea scouts too.

66
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Well, thanks, Tom, for your thoughts.

67
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Actually changed the wording to the post to be even more general and I think it helps.

68
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
It's important to understand: I'm not writing on behalf of the BSA

69
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
and I'm not exclusively writing or speaking to members of the BSA.

70
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So when I'm writing about scouting in general, as I was in this post,

71
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
not including ships and posts and varsity teams is pretty much a conscious choice.

72
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
When I deal with BSA-specific policy or programs,

73
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
I really do my best to do my research and write and speak with technical accuracy,

74
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
But when I'm writing about scouting in general, I want to use the simplest language possible.

75
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:07,000
If I attempted to include each of the specific organizational program names used around the world,

76
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
I'd end up with a pretty long list.

77
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
You know, in Canada they call ventures a venture company

78
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and they use the term venture scouts. in Australia, in the UK, Singapore, in Japan, in Brazil.

79
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
And then there's girl guides- and guides are not called scouts strictly-

80
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
and all the other groups in the scouting family.

81
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
There's Trail Life and the Baton Powell Service Corps and the navigators.

82
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
There's just tons and tons of terminology

83
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,000
And one way I think I can be useful is separating the basic principles of scouting

84
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
from that kind of well-worn organizational jargon that we're all familiar with.

85
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
I've been a loyal BSA scouter for more than 30 years,

86
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
but I'm attempting to write and to speak to scouts in general,

87
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Not just my fellow BSA scouts.

88
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
So thank you once again, Tom, for your thoughts.

89
