127
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,000
In this week's podcast, in Scoutmastership, in 7 Minutes or Less,

128
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
I want to try and offer you some guidance

129
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
if you're having some basic disagreements

130
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
with the way Scouting is being presented in the unit that you're a part of.

131
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
And then we've got an email question to answer

132
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And that's going to take up the rest of the podcast,

133
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
so let's get started. shall we

134
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Scoutmastership in 7 Minutes or Less.

135
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
From time to time, I hear from Scouters

136
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
who have some basic disagreement with the way things are going in their unit.

137
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Now, these disagreements aren't over things that endanger Scouts,

138
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
So let's be clear about that.

139
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Things that endanger Scouts have to be corrected

140
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
And you need to take positive action to do that.

141
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
The disagreements I'm speaking of

142
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
are generally around the application of the patrol system

143
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
or the way youth leadership is,

144
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
or maybe just kind of the general attitude

145
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
or the way meetings are conducted. you know

146
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
style and interpretation and application differences.

147
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
The basic scenario I see repeated time after time

148
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
is that someone in a supporting role

149
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
in a scout troop or a Cub Scout pack

150
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
or a venturing crew or whatever unit you're involved with,

151
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
is not happy with the way the folks in charge are doing things

152
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And, in a spirit of absolute goodwill,

153
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
they try to encourage changes

154
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
and they're either rebuffed or ignored.

155
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Now, adult roles in scouting are set up

156
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
so that there are certain people in charge of making decisions.

157
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Now, in terms of a scout troop,

158
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
that's what I'm most familiar with

159
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
In a scout troop, the scout master and the committee chair

160
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
and the chartered organization representative

161
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
are basically the decision makers.

162
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,000
It's not a democracy, it's a hierarchy,

163
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
and it's a hierarchy for a very good reason.

164
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Now, without wanting to digress into issues

165
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
that are going to cloud the question at hand,

166
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
naturally you would expect the decision makers

167
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
in a scouting unit to be interested in building consensus

168
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,000
and taking direction from that consensus,

169
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
not just ordering people around,

170
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
but very wisely. scouting is set up

171
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
so that there are decision makers,

172
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
because when it's time for decisions to be made,

173
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
it's got to come down to a group of people

174
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
or an individual who's going to make the final call.

175
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Now, if you aren't in a decision making position,

176
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
this can be very frustrating.

177
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
What do you do if you find yourself in a situation

178
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
where you have very basic disagreements

179
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
about the way things are being done

180
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
and you're not in one of those decision making positions?

181
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Should you just go with the flow,

182
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
or maybe you can just keep trying to influence change,

183
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
or maybe you can just make the changes yourself?

184
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Once you've talked these differences over

185
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
with the people who are making decisions

186
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
and you've offered your point of view,

187
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
and that point of view has been rejected,

188
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
you have two basic choices.

189
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
One is you're going to stay and play the game their way.

190
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
Two is you're going to go elsewhere.

191
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
There's no third option of staying and making things change.

192
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
If you aren't in a decision making role,

193
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
it's just not going to happen.

194
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
You're only going to make yourselves

195
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and other people unhappy.

196
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
When I tell people this,

197
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
sometimes they respond by telling me

198
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
that if they were to leave and to go elsewhere,

199
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
they would feel like they're abandoning scouts

200
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
to some inferior interpretation of scouting

201
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
and they feel that it's their job

202
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
to set everybody straight for the good of the scouts.

203
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Well, I'm going to say it again:

204
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Once you've clearly offered your vision and assistance

205
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
and you've received a no answer,

206
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:12,000
then it's time to take one of the two options:

207
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
Mounting an end, run around the decision makers.

208
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
that's not an option.

209
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
There's no third option, where you stay there

210
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and you institute the changes

211
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
despite the fact that people have clearly said no,

212
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
That's not what we're going to do.

213
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
It's not going to help.

214
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
It will have exactly the opposite effect.

215
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
Think about this for a moment

216
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Now. put yourself in the position of a manager

217
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
who has a dissatisfied employee

218
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
who wants to change the way things are run

219
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and decides to just unilaterally make those changes?

220
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Are you going to continue to employ that person?

221
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Probably not.

222
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Put yourself in the position of a head coach

223
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
of a baseball team

224
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
who has decided they know better than you,

225
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
so they're going to go change the lineup

226
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
without consulting you.

227
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
What would you do as that head coach?

228
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
I would get rid of the assistant coach,

229
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
because that's not the way the game works.

230
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
Now I've had people disagree

231
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
with the way that I work as a scout master,

232
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
and when we discuss those disagreements,

233
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
I explain what I'm doing,

234
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
and if they don't like it,

235
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
if they don't accept that answer,

236
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
further discussion on the subject

237
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
really becomes nothing less than complaining.

238
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Every once in a while, I've had a scouter

239
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
who mounts an end run

240
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
and tries to make changes that we've discussed

241
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and I've rejected.

242
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and I've got to tell you:

243
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
uncooperative volunteers don't last long.

244
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
they get frustrated or we ask them to leave.

245
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
It's an extraordinarily rare occurrence,

246
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
but when you come to loggerheads like this,

247
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
you've got to make decisions.

248
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:47,000
So let's remember what those two decisions are:

249
